# Reader expansion: from the raw samples to the reduced experiment

This note expands the probabilistic part of the universal limiting-Bayes
argument for arbitrary sample sizes

$$
n_1,n_2\ge 2.
$$

It starts from the raw normal samples and ends with the exact density

$$
\boxed{
f_\theta^Q(r,q)=
C_{a,b}\,
r^{a-1}(1-r)^{b-1}q^{1/2}
\frac{\theta^{b+3/2}(1-\theta)^{a+3/2}}
{\{2ar(1-\theta)+2b(1-r)\theta
       +q\theta(1-\theta)\}^{a+b+3/2}},
}
\tag{0.1}
$$

where

$$
a=\frac{n_1-1}{2},\qquad
b=\frac{n_2-1}{2}
\tag{0.2}
$$

and

$$
\boxed{
C_{a,b}=
\frac{2^{a+b+3/2}a^a b^b\Gamma(a+b+3/2)}
{\sqrt{2\pi}\,\Gamma(a)\Gamma(b)}.
}
\tag{0.3}
$$

For the smallest unequal pair \((n_1,n_2)=(2,3)\), this reduces to the
constant \(C_{1/2,1}=8/\pi\).

Nothing below uses the limiting-Bayes theorem.  The purpose is to make the
statistical reduction, all independence assertions, and every normalization
constant independently checkable.

## 1. The raw model and the sample-variance convention

We observe two independent normal samples

$$
X_{ij}\stackrel{\mathrm{iid}}{\sim}N(\mu,\sigma_i^2),
\qquad j=1,\ldots,n_i,\quad i=1,2,
\tag{1.1}
$$

where

$$
\mu\in\mathbb R,\qquad \sigma_1^2>0,\qquad \sigma_2^2>0.
$$

The two samples are independent of one another.  Throughout, \(S_i^2\) is
the usual **unbiased** sample variance:

$$
S_i^2=\frac1{n_i-1}
\sum_{j=1}^{n_i}(X_{ij}-\bar X_i)^2.
\tag{1.2}
$$

Consequently, the standard normal-sample theorem gives

$$
U_i:=\frac{(n_i-1)S_i^2}{\sigma_i^2}
\sim\chi^2_{n_i-1}.
\tag{1.3}
$$

This convention matters.  If one instead defined a sample variance with
denominator \(n_i\), the scaling factors below would change.

Put

$$
D=\bar X_2-\bar X_1,\qquad
Y_i=\frac{S_i^2}{n_i},
\tag{1.4}
$$

and, whenever \(Y_1+Y_2>0\),

$$
r=\frac{Y_1}{Y_1+Y_2},\qquad
q=\frac{D^2}{Y_1+Y_2}.
\tag{1.5}
$$

### Null-set convention

Since \(n_i-1>0\), a \(\chi^2_{n_i-1}\) variable is strictly positive almost
surely.  Equation (1.3) therefore implies

$$
\mathbb P(S_i^2=0)=0.
$$

Thus \(Y_1,Y_2>0\) almost surely and

$$
0<r<1
$$

almost surely.  We will also see that \(D\sim N(0,\tau)\) with \(\tau>0\);
hence \(\mathbb P(D=0)=0\), so

$$
q>0
$$

almost surely.  We may define \(r\) and \(q\) arbitrarily on the null sets
where their displayed formulas are undefined.  Such definitions do not
change any probability or risk.

The value \(q=0\), used later in the analytic contradiction, is therefore a
**boundary value reached by continuity**, not an event having positive
probability.

## 2. The unequal-size Graybill--Deal weight

The usual Graybill--Deal estimator weights each sample mean by the reciprocal
of its estimated variance:

$$
\delta_{\mathrm{GD}}
=
\frac{(n_1/S_1^2)\bar X_1+(n_2/S_2^2)\bar X_2}
{n_1/S_1^2+n_2/S_2^2}.
\tag{2.1}
$$

Since \(Y_i=S_i^2/n_i\), equation (2.1) is

$$
\delta_{\mathrm{GD}}
=
\frac{Y_1^{-1}\bar X_1+Y_2^{-1}\bar X_2}
{Y_1^{-1}+Y_2^{-1}}.
$$

The coefficient of \(\bar X_2\) is

$$
\frac{Y_2^{-1}}{Y_1^{-1}+Y_2^{-1}}
=\frac{Y_1}{Y_1+Y_2}
=r.
\tag{2.2}
$$

The coefficient of \(\bar X_1\) is \(1-r\).  Therefore, for arbitrary
possibly unequal sample sizes,

$$
\boxed{
\delta_{\mathrm{GD}}
=(1-r)\bar X_1+r\bar X_2
=\bar X_1+rD.
}
\tag{2.3}
$$

So Graybill--Deal uses the random quantity \(r\) as an estimate of an
unknown ideal weight.

## 3. Reparametrizing the two variances

The variances of the sample means are

$$
v_1=\operatorname{Var}(\bar X_1)=\frac{\sigma_1^2}{n_1},
\qquad
v_2=\operatorname{Var}(\bar X_2)=\frac{\sigma_2^2}{n_2}.
\tag{3.1}
$$

Define

$$
\tau=v_1+v_2,\qquad
\theta=\frac{v_1}{v_1+v_2}=\frac{v_1}{\tau}.
\tag{3.2}
$$

Because \(v_1,v_2>0\),

$$
\tau>0,\qquad 0<\theta<1.
$$

Conversely,

$$
v_1=\theta\tau,\qquad v_2=(1-\theta)\tau,
\tag{3.3}
$$

and hence

$$
\sigma_1^2=n_1\theta\tau,\qquad
\sigma_2^2=n_2(1-\theta)\tau.
\tag{3.4}
$$

Thus \((\tau,\theta)\in(0,\infty)\times(0,1)\) is simply another
one-to-one parametrization of the two positive variances:

* \(\tau\) is their overall scale on the sample-mean scale;
* \(\theta\) is the ratio governing the optimal weight.

Since the sample means are independent,

$$
D=\bar X_2-\bar X_1
\sim N(0,v_1+v_2)
=N(0,\tau).
\tag{3.5}
$$

Therefore

$$
D_0=\frac D{\sqrt\tau}\sim N(0,1),
\qquad
W=D_0^2=\frac{D^2}{\tau}\sim\chi^2_1.
\tag{3.6}
$$

In particular,

$$
\mathbb E_\theta W=1.
\tag{3.7}
$$

## 4. The known-variance oracle

Define

$$
M=\bar X_1+\theta D
=(1-\theta)\bar X_1+\theta\bar X_2.
\tag{4.1}
$$

This is the inverse-variance estimator that would be used if \(v_1,v_2\)
were known.  Indeed, the inverse-variance coefficient on \(\bar X_2\) is

$$
\frac{v_2^{-1}}{v_1^{-1}+v_2^{-1}}
=\frac{v_1}{v_1+v_2}
=\theta.
\tag{4.2}
$$

Let

$$
A=\bar X_1-\mu,\qquad B=\bar X_2-\mu.
$$

Then \(A\sim N(0,v_1)\), \(B\sim N(0,v_2)\), and \(A\) and \(B\) are
independent.  Also

$$
D=B-A
$$

and the oracle error is

$$
E=M-\mu=A+\theta(B-A)=(1-\theta)A+\theta B.
\tag{4.3}
$$

Its covariance with \(D\) is

$$
\begin{aligned}
\operatorname{Cov}(E,D)
&=\operatorname{Cov}((1-\theta)A+\theta B,\ B-A)\\
&=-(1-\theta)\operatorname{Var}(A)
  +\theta\operatorname{Var}(B)\\
&=-(1-\theta)v_1+\theta v_2.
\end{aligned}
\tag{4.4}
$$

Using \(v_1=\theta\tau\) and \(v_2=(1-\theta)\tau\),

$$
\operatorname{Cov}(E,D)
=-(1-\theta)\theta\tau
+\theta(1-\theta)\tau
=0.
\tag{4.5}
$$

The pair \((E,D)\) is jointly normal because it is a linear transformation of
the jointly normal pair \((A,B)\).  Jointly normal random variables with zero
covariance are independent.  Hence

$$
E\perp D.
\tag{4.6}
$$

Its variance is

$$
\begin{aligned}
\operatorname{Var}(E)
&=(1-\theta)^2v_1+\theta^2v_2\\
&=(1-\theta)^2\theta\tau
  +\theta^2(1-\theta)\tau\\
&=\tau\theta(1-\theta)\{(1-\theta)+\theta\}\\
&=\tau\theta(1-\theta).
\end{aligned}
\tag{4.7}
$$

For a normal sample, the sample mean is independent of the sample variance.
Using this fact within each sample and independence between samples,

$$
(\bar X_1,\bar X_2)\perp(S_1^2,S_2^2).
\tag{4.8}
$$

Therefore \((E,D)\), which is a function of the two means, is independent
of \((S_1^2,S_2^2)\).  Combining this with \(E\perp D\) gives

$$
\boxed{
E\perp(D,S_1^2,S_2^2).
}
\tag{4.9}
$$

One can verify the last regrouping directly from densities:

$$
\begin{aligned}
f_{E,D,S_1^2,S_2^2}
&=f_{E,D}\,f_{S_1^2,S_2^2}\\
&=f_E\,f_D\,f_{S_1^2,S_2^2}\\
&=f_E\,f_{D,S_1^2,S_2^2}.
\end{aligned}
$$

The final factorization is precisely (4.9).

## 5. Exact risk decomposition

Let \(\phi=\phi(r,q)\) be any measurable real-valued weight for which the
displayed risk is finite, and define

$$
\delta_\phi=\bar X_1+D\phi(r,q).
\tag{5.1}
$$

Since \(M=\bar X_1+\theta D\),

$$
\delta_\phi-\mu
=E+D\{\phi(r,q)-\theta\}.
\tag{5.2}
$$

Squaring,

$$
\begin{aligned}
(\delta_\phi-\mu)^2
={}&E^2
+2ED\{\phi(r,q)-\theta\}\\
&+D^2\{\phi(r,q)-\theta\}^2.
\end{aligned}
\tag{5.3}
$$

The cross term has expectation zero.  Indeed,
\(D\{\phi(r,q)-\theta\}\) is a function of
\((D,S_1^2,S_2^2)\), whereas \(E\) is independent of that entire vector and
has mean zero.  Thus

$$
\mathbb E\!\left[ED\{\phi(r,q)-\theta\}\right]
=\mathbb E[E]\,
 \mathbb E\!\left[D\{\phi(r,q)-\theta\}\right]
=0.
$$

Using (4.7) and \(D^2=\tau D_0^2\), we obtain

$$
\boxed{
R_{\mu,\tau,\theta}(\delta_\phi)
=
\tau\theta(1-\theta)
+\tau\,
\mathbb E_\theta\!\left[
D_0^2\{\phi(r,q)-\theta\}^2
\right].
}
\tag{5.4}
$$

The first term is the oracle risk and is the same for every \(\phi\).  All
comparisons between estimators of the form (5.1) reduce to the second term.
For Graybill--Deal, \(\phi(r,q)=r\).

## 6. The risk-weighted probability law \(Q_\theta\)

Let

$$
\mathcal Z=(0,1)\times(0,\infty),
$$

the almost-sure range of \((r,q)\).  For a Borel set
\(C\subseteq\mathcal Z\), define

$$
Q_\theta(C)
=
\mathbb E_\theta\!\left[
D_0^2\,\mathbf 1_{\{(r,q)\in C\}}
\right].
\tag{6.1}
$$

This is a probability measure:

1. it is nonnegative;
2. countable additivity follows from monotone convergence;
3. by (3.7),

   $$
   Q_\theta(\mathcal Z)=\mathbb E_\theta[D_0^2]=1.
   $$

More generally, for every nonnegative measurable \(g\),

$$
\int_{\mathcal Z}g(r,q)\,Q_\theta(d r\,d q)
=
\mathbb E_\theta[D_0^2g(r,q)].
\tag{6.2}
$$

This follows first for indicators by definition, then for nonnegative simple
functions by linearity, and finally for arbitrary nonnegative measurable
functions by monotone convergence.

Applying (6.2) to \(g(r,q)=\{\phi(r,q)-\theta\}^2\), equation (5.4) becomes

$$
\boxed{
R_{\mu,\tau,\theta}(\delta_\phi)
=
\tau\theta(1-\theta)
+\tau\int
\{\phi(r,q)-\theta\}^2\,Q_\theta(d r\,d q).
}
\tag{6.3}
$$

Thus the reduced problem is an ordinary decision problem:

* parameter: \(\theta\in(0,1)\);
* observation: \((r,q)\sim Q_\theta\);
* action: the reported value \(\phi(r,q)\);
* loss: squared error \((\phi-\theta)^2\).

This explains why \(Q_\theta\) is introduced: it absorbs the factor \(D_0^2\)
already present in the original risk.  It is not an arbitrary change of
model.

### Why reduced domination implies full domination

Suppose \(\psi(r,q)\) satisfies

$$
\int(\psi-\theta)^2\,dQ_\theta
\le
\int(r-\theta)^2\,dQ_\theta
\qquad(0<\theta<1),
\tag{6.4}
$$

with strict inequality for at least one \(\theta\).  Multiplying by
\(\tau>0\) and adding the common oracle term in (6.3) shows that

$$
\bar X_1+D\psi(r,q)
$$

dominates Graybill--Deal in the original normal problem.  This holds for
every \(\mu\) and \(\tau>0\).  Equation (3.4) shows that all positive variance
pairs are covered.

We only need to find a dominator inside the subclass (5.1).  Allowing all
possible estimators in the original problem cannot restore admissibility
after a dominator has been found in this smaller subclass.

## 7. Removing the nuisance location and scale

The common mean \(\mu\) does not enter the law of \(D\), the sample
variances, \(r\), or \(q\).

Define the normalized variables

$$
G_1=\frac{Y_1}{\tau},\qquad
G_2=\frac{Y_2}{\tau},\qquad
W=\frac{D^2}{\tau}=D_0^2.
\tag{7.1}
$$

Then

$$
r=\frac{G_1}{G_1+G_2},
\qquad
q=\frac{W}{G_1+G_2}.
\tag{7.2}
$$

Therefore the distribution of \((r,q)\) is unchanged if we set
\(\tau=1\).  After division by \(\tau\), it depends only on \(\theta\).

This is the precise meaning of â€œnormalize \(\tau=1\).â€  It does not assume
special values of the original variances; it applies an invertible scale
change to the variables.

## 8. The component laws for arbitrary \(n_1,n_2\)

Recall

$$
a=\frac{n_1-1}{2},\qquad
b=\frac{n_2-1}{2},
$$

so \(n_1-1=2a\) and \(n_2-1=2b\).  From (1.3),

$$
U_1\sim\chi^2_{2a},
\qquad
U_2\sim\chi^2_{2b}.
\tag{8.1}
$$

Solving (1.3) for \(S_i^2\),

$$
S_1^2=\frac{\sigma_1^2}{2a}U_1,
\qquad
S_2^2=\frac{\sigma_2^2}{2b}U_2.
\tag{8.2}
$$

Hence

$$
\begin{aligned}
G_1
&=\frac{S_1^2}{n_1\tau}
=\frac{\sigma_1^2}{n_1\tau}\frac{U_1}{2a}
=\theta\frac{U_1}{2a},
\\[1ex]
G_2
&=\frac{S_2^2}{n_2\tau}
=\frac{\sigma_2^2}{n_2\tau}\frac{U_2}{2b}
=(1-\theta)\frac{U_2}{2b}.
\end{aligned}
\tag{8.3}
$$

A \(\chi^2_{2a}\) variable is

$$
\operatorname{Gamma}(a,\text{ scale }2).
$$

Dividing it by \(2a\) gives

$$
\operatorname{Gamma}\left(a,\text{ scale }\frac1a\right).
$$

Therefore

$$
\boxed{
G_1\sim\operatorname{Gamma}
\left(a,\text{ scale }\frac{\theta}{a}\right),
\qquad
G_2\sim\operatorname{Gamma}
\left(b,\text{ scale }\frac{1-\theta}{b}\right).
}
\tag{8.4}
$$

For reference, a Gamma variable with shape \(\alpha\) and scale \(s\) has
density

$$
\frac{x^{\alpha-1}e^{-x/s}}
{\Gamma(\alpha)s^\alpha},
\qquad x>0.
\tag{8.5}
$$

Consequently,

$$
f_{G_1}(g_1)
=
\frac{a^a}{\Gamma(a)\theta^a}
g_1^{a-1}
\exp\!\left(-\frac{ag_1}{\theta}\right),
\qquad g_1>0,
\tag{8.6}
$$

and

$$
f_{G_2}(g_2)
=
\frac{b^b}{\Gamma(b)(1-\theta)^b}
g_2^{b-1}
\exp\!\left(-\frac{bg_2}{1-\theta}\right),
\qquad g_2>0.
\tag{8.7}
$$

From (3.6),

$$
W\sim\chi^2_1,
$$

with density

$$
f_W(w)
=
\frac1{\sqrt{2\pi}}w^{-1/2}e^{-w/2},
\qquad w>0.
\tag{8.8}
$$

The variables \(G_1,G_2,W\) are mutually independent:

* \(G_1\) and \(G_2\) are functions of different independent samples;
* \(W\) is a function of the two sample means;
* the vector of sample means is independent of the vector of sample
  variances in the normal model.

## 9. What the \(Q_\theta\) weighting does

It is convenient temporarily to extend the weighting defining \(Q_\theta\)
to the full triple \((G_1,G_2,W)\), and then take the distribution of
\((r,q)\).

The weight is \(W\), so the \(W\)-density becomes

$$
f_W^Q(w)
=w f_W(w)
=\frac1{\sqrt{2\pi}}w^{1/2}e^{-w/2}.
\tag{9.1}
$$

This is normalized because

$$
\int_0^\infty w f_W(w)\,dw=\mathbb E W=1.
$$

It is also exactly the density of \(\chi^2_3\):

$$
\frac1{2^{3/2}\Gamma(3/2)}w^{3/2-1}e^{-w/2}
=
\frac1{\sqrt{2\pi}}w^{1/2}e^{-w/2},
\tag{9.2}
$$

because \(\Gamma(3/2)=\sqrt\pi/2\).  Thus the risk weighting has the simple
effect

$$
\chi^2_1\longmapsto\chi^2_3.
$$

Since \(W\) was independent of \((G_1,G_2)\), and the weight depends only on
\(W\), the joint weighted density remains the product

$$
f_{G_1}(g_1)f_{G_2}(g_2)f_W^Q(w).
\tag{9.3}
$$

Substitution of (8.6), (8.7), and (9.1) gives

$$
\begin{aligned}
&\frac{a^a b^b}
{\sqrt{2\pi}\,\Gamma(a)\Gamma(b)
 \theta^a(1-\theta)^b}
g_1^{a-1}g_2^{b-1}w^{1/2}\\
&\qquad{}\times
\exp\!\left\{
-\frac{ag_1}{\theta}
-\frac{bg_2}{1-\theta}
-\frac w2
\right\}.
\end{aligned}
\tag{9.4}
$$

## 10. The change of variables \((G_1,G_2,W)\mapsto(r,q,t)\)

Put

$$
t=G_1+G_2.
\tag{10.1}
$$

The inverse transformation is

$$
G_1=rt,\qquad
G_2=(1-r)t,\qquad
W=qt,
\tag{10.2}
$$

on the domain

$$
0<r<1,\qquad q>0,\qquad t>0.
$$

It is smooth and one-to-one between the positive domains.  Using columns in
the order \((r,q,t)\),

$$
\frac{\partial(G_1,G_2,W)}{\partial(r,q,t)}
=
\begin{pmatrix}
t&0&r\\
-t&0&1-r\\
0&t&q
\end{pmatrix}.
\tag{10.3}
$$

Its determinant is \(-t^2\), so the absolute Jacobian is

$$
\left|
\frac{\partial(G_1,G_2,W)}{\partial(r,q,t)}
\right|
=t^2.
\tag{10.4}
$$

The power factors in (9.4) become

$$
\begin{aligned}
G_1^{a-1}G_2^{b-1}W^{1/2}
&=(rt)^{a-1}\{(1-r)t\}^{b-1}(qt)^{1/2}\\
&=r^{a-1}(1-r)^{b-1}q^{1/2}
t^{a+b-3/2}.
\end{aligned}
\tag{10.5}
$$

Multiplication by the Jacobian \(t^2\) changes the \(t\)-power to

$$
t^{a+b+1/2}.
\tag{10.6}
$$

Define

$$
p=a+b+\frac32.
\tag{10.7}
$$

Then \(a+b+1/2=p-1\), so the power is \(t^{p-1}\).

The exponent in (9.4) becomes

$$
-tA_\theta(r,q),
$$

where

$$
A_\theta(r,q)
=\frac{ar}{\theta}
+\frac{b(1-r)}{1-\theta}
+\frac q2.
\tag{10.8}
$$

Hence the joint weighted density of \((r,q,t)\) is

$$
\begin{aligned}
&
\frac{a^a b^b}
{\sqrt{2\pi}\,\Gamma(a)\Gamma(b)
 \theta^a(1-\theta)^b}\\
&\quad{}\times
r^{a-1}(1-r)^{b-1}q^{1/2}
t^{p-1}e^{-tA_\theta(r,q)}.
\end{aligned}
\tag{10.9}
$$

## 11. Integrating out \(t\)

Since \(A_\theta(r,q)>0\),

$$
\int_0^\infty t^{p-1}e^{-At}\,dt
=\frac{\Gamma(p)}{A^p}.
\tag{11.1}
$$

This is the defining Gamma integral after the substitution \(u=At\).
Integrating (10.9) over \(t\) gives

$$
\begin{aligned}
f_\theta^Q(r,q)
={}&
\frac{a^a b^b\Gamma(p)}
{\sqrt{2\pi}\,\Gamma(a)\Gamma(b)
 \theta^a(1-\theta)^b}\\
&\quad{}\times
r^{a-1}(1-r)^{b-1}q^{1/2}
A_\theta(r,q)^{-p}.
\end{aligned}
\tag{11.2}
$$

Put \(A_\theta\) over the common denominator
\(2\theta(1-\theta)\):

$$
A_\theta(r,q)
=
\frac{
2ar(1-\theta)+2b(1-r)\theta
  +q\theta(1-\theta)}
{2\theta(1-\theta)}.
\tag{11.3}
$$

Define

$$
B_\theta(r,q)
=
2ar(1-\theta)+2b(1-r)\theta
  +q\theta(1-\theta).
\tag{11.4}
$$

Then

$$
A_\theta(r,q)^{-p}
=
\frac{2^p\theta^p(1-\theta)^p}
{B_\theta(r,q)^p}.
\tag{11.5}
$$

Substituting into (11.2), the powers of \(\theta\) are

$$
\theta^{p-a}
=\theta^{b+3/2}
\tag{11.6}
$$

and

$$
(1-\theta)^{p-b}
=(1-\theta)^{a+3/2}.
\tag{11.7}
$$

Therefore

$$
\boxed{
\begin{aligned}
f_\theta^Q(r,q)
={}&
\frac{2^p a^a b^b\Gamma(p)}
{\sqrt{2\pi}\,\Gamma(a)\Gamma(b)}
r^{a-1}(1-r)^{b-1}q^{1/2}\\
&\times
\frac{\theta^{b+3/2}(1-\theta)^{a+3/2}}
{\{2ar(1-\theta)+2b(1-r)\theta
  +q\theta(1-\theta)\}^{p}},
\end{aligned}
}
\tag{11.8}
$$

where \(p=a+b+3/2\).  This is exactly (0.1)--(0.3).

The â€œcrossedâ€ exponents

$$
\theta^{b+3/2}(1-\theta)^{a+3/2}
$$

are not typographical errors.  They arise from subtracting the original
\(\theta^{-a}(1-\theta)^{-b}\) Gamma normalizers from the factor
\(\{\theta(1-\theta)\}^p\) created when \(A_\theta^{-p}\) is simplified.

## 12. Why the density is normalized and everywhere positive

There is no need to perform the difficult double integral in \(r\) and
\(q\).  Normalization follows from the construction:

1. the densities in (8.6), (8.7), and (9.1) each integrate to one;
2. their product (9.3) is therefore a probability density;
3. the bijective change of variables (10.2), using the exact Jacobian
   (10.4), preserves total mass;
4. integrating out \(t\) preserves the remaining total mass.

Thus (11.8) integrates to one.

It is strictly positive for every

$$
0<\theta<1,\qquad 0<r<1,\qquad q>0.
$$

Indeed every factor in its numerator is positive, while

$$
B_\theta(r,q)
=
2ar(1-\theta)+2b(1-r)\theta
  +q\theta(1-\theta)>0.
$$

The reduced experiment is consequently dominated by two-dimensional
Lebesgue measure \(dr\,dq\), and its density is everywhere positive on the
open observation space.  These are the density facts needed for the
limiting-Bayes complete-class theorem.

Also, if \(N\subseteq\mathcal Z\) is null for the ordinary distribution of
\((r,q)\), then it remains null after risk weighting:

$$
Q_\theta(N)
=\mathbb E_\theta[
W\mathbf 1_{\{(r,q)\in N\}}]
=0.
$$

Thus the null-set conventions made in Section 1 remain harmless under
\(Q_\theta\).

## 13. Check at \((n_1,n_2)=(2,3)\): why the constant is \(8/\pi\)

For \(n_1=2,n_2=3\),

$$
a=\frac12,\qquad b=1,\qquad p=3.
$$

The general constant becomes

$$
\begin{aligned}
C_{1/2,1}
&=
\frac{2^3(1/2)^{1/2}1^1\Gamma(3)}
{\sqrt{2\pi}\,\Gamma(1/2)\Gamma(1)}\\
&=
\frac{8(1/\sqrt2)(2)}
{\sqrt{2\pi}(\sqrt\pi)(1)}\\
&=
\frac{8\sqrt2}{\sqrt2\,\pi}\\
&=\frac8\pi.
\end{aligned}
\tag{13.1}
$$

Moreover,

$$
r^{a-1}(1-r)^{b-1}=r^{-1/2},
$$

and

$$
B_\theta(r,q)
=r(1-\theta)+2(1-r)\theta+q\theta(1-\theta).
$$

Thus (11.8) reduces to

$$
\boxed{
f_\theta^Q(r,q)
=
\frac8\pi\frac{q^{1/2}}{\sqrt r}\,
\frac{\theta^{5/2}(1-\theta)^2}
{\{r(1-\theta)+2(1-r)\theta
  +q\theta(1-\theta)\}^3}.
}
\tag{13.2}
$$

## 14. Compact map of the reduction

The probabilistic chain is

$$
\begin{aligned}
\text{raw normal samples}
&\longrightarrow
(D,Y_1,Y_2)\\
&\longrightarrow
(W,G_1,G_2)\\
&\xrightarrow{\text{weight by }W}
Q_\theta\\
&\longrightarrow
(r,q).
\end{aligned}
$$

At the risk level,

$$
\begin{aligned}
R(\bar X_1+D\phi)
&=
\underbrace{\tau\theta(1-\theta)}
_{\text{common oracle risk}}\\
&\quad+
\underbrace{\tau
\int(\phi-\theta)^2\,dQ_\theta}
_{\text{part depending on }\phi}.
\end{aligned}
$$

Therefore, proving that \(r\) is inadmissible for estimating \(\theta\) in
the \(Q_\theta\)-experiment is sufficient to prove that the original
Graybill--Deal estimator is inadmissible.

## 15. WolframAlpha / computer-algebra checklist

The following inputs check the main algebra.  WolframAlpha generally accepts
either the plain-language descriptions or the Mathematica-style inputs.

### Oracle covariance

```text
simplify -(1-theta)*(theta*tau) + theta*((1-theta)*tau)
```

Result: \(0\).

### Oracle variance

```text
simplify (1-theta)^2*(theta*tau)
       + theta^2*((1-theta)*tau)
```

Result: \(\theta(1-\theta)\tau\).

### Unequal-size Graybill--Deal coefficient

```text
simplify (1/y2)/(1/y1+1/y2)
```

Result: \(y_1/(y_1+y_2)\).

### Gamma scaling

Ask for

```text
chi square distribution with 2a degrees of freedom pdf
```

and substitute \(u=2ag/\theta\), including the derivative
\(du/dg=2a/\theta\).  The resulting density is (8.6).

Equivalently, use:

```text
If U ~ Gamma(shape a, scale 2),
distribution of theta U/(2a)
```

The answer is
\(\operatorname{Gamma}(a,\text{ scale }\theta/a)\).

### Risk weighting

Ask for the \(\chi^2_1\) and \(\chi^2_3\) densities.  Multiplying the
\(\chi^2_1\) density by \(w\) gives the \(\chi^2_3\) density because

```text
Gamma(3/2)
```

returns \(\sqrt\pi/2\).

### Jacobian

```text
det {{t,0,r},{-t,0,1-r},{0,t,q}}
```

Result: \(-t^2\), whose absolute value is \(t^2\).

### Power of \(t\)

```text
simplify (a-1)+(b-1)+1/2+2
```

Result: \(a+b+1/2=p-1\).

### Gamma integral

```text
integrate t^(p-1) exp(-A t)
from t=0 to infinity, assuming A>0 and p>0
```

Result: \(\Gamma(p)/A^p\).

### Common denominator in the exponent

```text
together a*r/theta + b*(1-r)/(1-theta) + q/2
```

Result:

$$
\frac{2ar(1-\theta)+2b(1-r)\theta
      +q\theta(1-\theta)}
{2\theta(1-\theta)}.
$$

### General normalization constant

With \(p=a+b+3/2\), simplify

```text
(a^a*b^b /
 (sqrt(2*pi)*Gamma(a)*Gamma(b)*theta^a*(1-theta)^b))
* Gamma(p) * (2*theta*(1-theta))^p
```

The result is

$$
\frac{2^p a^a b^b\Gamma(p)}
{\sqrt{2\pi}\Gamma(a)\Gamma(b)}
\theta^{b+3/2}(1-\theta)^{a+3/2}.
$$

### The special constant \(8/\pi\)

```text
simplify
2^3*(1/2)^(1/2)*Gamma(3) /
(sqrt(2*pi)*Gamma(1/2)*Gamma(1))
```

Result: \(8/\pi\).
